body:
![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Instead of spawning an external `curl` process via `execFileAsync`, which introduces external platform dependencies and process overhead, leverage the native `fetch` API available in Node.js 18+. This improves portability (especially on Windows or minimal Docker environments where `curl` might not be installed) and simplifies error handling.

```javascript
async function defaultDownload(row) {
  const url = validateSourceAuthority(row);
  const response = await fetch(url.href, {
    headers: {
      'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; CIE-Copilot-Source-Audit/1.0; bounded-public-document-retrieval)',
      'Referer': 'https://pastpapers.papacambridge.com/',
      'Accept': 'application/pdf,application/octet-stream;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.1',
    },
    signal: AbortSignal.timeout(60000),
  });
  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error("Failed to download document: " + response.status + " " + response.statusText);
  }
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
  return {
    bytes: Buffer.from(arrayBuffer),
    http_status: response.status,
    content_type: response.headers.get('content-type') || 'application/pdf',
    final_url: response.url || url.href,
  };
}
```

